Article 20

The Regional Government provides facilities for the fulfillment of children's rights in the family environment and alternative environment care, by: a. providing or facilitating alternative care for children whose parents have died or are sick so that it is not possible to care for the child;

b. providing welfare support to all children; c. fulfilling the rights of child growth and development and protecting children with social welfare problems and children in conflict with the law; d. protecting children from cruel, inhuman treatment and degrading human dignity;

e. conducting training for parents on good parenting or establishing consultation institutions for families;

f. playing an active role in helping families carry out their obligations;

g. preventing marriage at child age; and h. providing child-friendly infrastructure, child-friendly play areas and safe routes to school.

Article 35

(1) The business sector has responsibilities in the implementation of women's empowerment and child protection.

(2) The form of business sector responsibility as referred to in paragraph (4) is carried out in the form of:

a. financial assistance to service institutions;
b. empowerment;
c. capacity building;
d. publication and information media;
e. provision of facilities and infrastructure;
f. establishment of special institutions for prevention and violence complaint services; and
g. non-binding social assistance.

Article 31

(1) In order to implement the fulfillment of child-friendly rights and various laws and regulations as well as policies related to the fulfillment of children's rights and special protection of children, the Regional Government, Private Sector and Community Institutions make efforts to realize a Child-Friendly Regency.

(2) To realize a Child-Friendly Regency as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government, Private Sector, and Community Institutions pay attention to children's rights related to the right to life, growth and development, protection, and participation.

(3) The Regional Government, Private Sector, and Community Institutions in realizing a Child-Friendly Regency are incorporated in the Child-Friendly Regency task force. (4) The Child-Friendly Regency task force as referred to in paragraph (3) is responsible for the fulfillment of children's rights related to: a. civil rights and freedoms;

b. family environment and alternative care;

c. basic health and welfare;

d. education, use of leisure time, and arts and culture activities; and e. special protection of children.

(5) Further provisions regarding the Child-Friendly Regency as referred to in paragraph (1) are regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 15

Every Child has the right to obtain Civil Rights and Freedoms as referred to in Article 14 letter a in the form of:

a. birth certificate;
b. child identity card.
c. expressing opinions according to age and level of intelligence;
d. obtaining healthy and safe information;
e. freedom to assemble and organize that is appropriate for them; f. maintenance of reputation and not being exploited to the public without the child's permission; and/or
g. participating in development through children's forums at the village/sub-district, sub-district and even District levels.

Article 27

(1) The Regional Government is responsible for realizing Child Protection in the Region in the form of coordinating the implementation of the administration of Child Protection in the Region.

(2) The coordination of the implementation of the administration of Child Protection in the Region as referred to in paragraph (1) is in order to fulfill the Rights of Children who require Special Protection.

(3) Children who require Special Protection as referred to in paragraph (1) include:

a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are exploited economically and/or sexually; e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children who are perpetrators of pornography;
h. children with human immunodeficiency virus/acquired immuno deficiency syndrome;
i. children victims of abduction, sale, and/or trafficking; j. children victims of physical and/or psychological violence;
k. children victims of sexual crimes;
l. children victims of terrorism networks;
m. children perpetrators of terrorism networks:
n. children with disabilities;
o. children victims of mistreatment and neglect;
p. children with deviant social behavior; and
q. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.

