a. civil rights and freedoms;
b. family environment and alternative care;
c. basic health and welfare;
d. education, leisure time utilization and cultural activities; and e. special protection.

Article 19

In fulfilling rights in the family environment and alternative care, the Family:

a. provides parenting, affection, attention, protection, facilities, maintains family harmony, always considering what is best for the child, according to the condition and capacity of the parents;
b. prevents marriage at child age and keeps the child from being trapped in promiscuity, permissive culture, and avoids narcotics, HIV and AIDS and is protected from pornography;
c. provides religious education and instills noble values from an early age to the child;
d. provides national insight, heroism and national defense from an early age to the child; and
e. provides welfare support.

Article 18

Every Child has the right to obtain rights in the family environment and alternative care as referred to in Article 14 letter b in the form of:

a. receiving priority to be raised by their own parents;
b. not being separated from their parents, except such separation is in the best interests of the child;
c. receiving good, polite and loving parenting; d. receiving balanced parenting from both parents; e. receiving welfare support even though their parents are unable;
f. receiving alternative care in the event both parents die or suffer from an illness that does not allow them to care for the child; and
g. receiving family harmony.

Article 26

The Regional Government provides facilities to fulfill the Right to Education, Use of Leisure Time and Cultural Activities, by:

a. providing the widest possible opportunity for children to obtain education by ensuring the implementation of a 12 (twelve) year compulsory education program for all children free of charge;
b. providing opportunities and accessibility for children with disabilities to obtain education;
c. organizing inclusive education for children with disabilities; d. optimizing skills education;
e. providing city parks, playgrounds, art buildings and sports arenas as a forum to hone and develop children's talents, interests and creativity in the fields of arts, culture and sports;
f. realizing child-friendly schools at every level of education; g. providing scholarships for students from underprivileged families;
h. facilitating school dropouts at Open Schools or Community Learning Activity Centers;
i. providing awards to students who excel in academics, arts, culture and sports;
j. issuing policies for education providers not to expel students from educational institutions, by conducting guidance, except involved in criminal acts;
k. organizing an educational and non-violent student orientation period; and
l. establishing a children's creativity center.

