CONCERNING

IMPLEMENTATION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN EMPOWERMENT AND PROTECTION

A. GENERAL ELUCIDATION

Pancasila and the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia have firmly and clearly provided protection for Human Rights, including the rights of women and children. Every person has equal dignity which is not only guaranteed by the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia, but has also been internationally recognized through the Universal Declaration of Human Rights (UDHR) of 1948 which explicitly regulates the importance of protection of Human Rights including the protection of women and children.

To ensure the fulfillment of the rights of women and children, regional governments are obligated and responsible to implement and support national policies in the administration of governmental affairs in the field of women and children empowerment and protection in the region. In addition, regional governments are also given the obligation to supervise the implementation of women and children empowerment and protection.

The implementation of women and children empowerment and protection is an effort to realize capable women and an effort to protect women and children from all acts of violence in order to achieve the goal of welfare. Women and children empowerment and protection that has been implemented so far has not been optimal and comprehensive in reaching the problems faced by women and children. In addition, public awareness of efforts to empower and protect women and children is still very lacking. To achieve these objectives and to implement the authority of regional governments as outlined in Law

Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government in the sub-affairs of women and children empowerment and protection, a regulation is needed that can serve as a legal umbrella for regional governments in the administration of governmental affairs in the field of women empowerment and child protection in the region.

In formulating policies related to women empowerment and child protection, it is necessary to pay attention to the principles of good legislation formation as regulated in Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning

Formulation of Legislation include:

r. clarity of purpose;
s. appropriate institution or forming official;
t. conformity between type, hierarchy, and content material;
u. enforceability;
v. efficiency and effectiveness;
w. clarity of formulation; and
x. openness.

In addition to the principles of legislation formation, it is also necessary to pay attention to the principles of content material which include:

a. protection;
b. humanity;
c. nationality;
d. kinship;
e. archipelago;
f. unity in diversity;
g. justice;
h. equality of position in law and government; i. order and legal certainty; and/or
j. balance, harmony, and congruity.

The implementation of governmental affairs in the field of Women Empowerment and Child Protection aims to increase efforts to Protect women's rights, fulfill children's rights and Special Protection for Children and to increase the role of Regional Government and the business world, media and the community in efforts to Empower Women and Protect Children. The main material contained in the body of the Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Women and Children Empowerment and Protection includes:

a. fulfillment of women's rights;
b. women empowerment;
c. women protection; d. child protection;
e. fulfillment of children's rights;
f. integrated service center; g. safe house;
h. child-friendly regency;
i. guidance and supervision; j. community participation;
k. awards; and
l. financing.
B. ELUCIDATION ARTICLE BY ARTICLE Article 1 Sufficiently clear

Article 33

(1) The form of guidance as referred to in Article 32 may be carried out through activities:

a. providing socialization to the community and business sector regarding policies on women's empowerment and child protection; b. providing books, leaflets, brochures regarding women's empowerment and child protection, and other related issues and disseminating them to the community;
c. facilitating capacity building for women's empowerment and child protection institutions;
d. facilitating women's participation forums and women's and children's organizations;
e. facilitating rehabilitation and reintegration services for women and children victims of discrimination, violence and disasters;
f. coordinating the participation of the business sector and other institutions, including international assistance for women's empowerment and child protection; and
g. providing awards to the community and business sector both individually and institutionally who have made efforts for women's empowerment and child protection.
(2) The form of supervision as referred to in Article 32 may be in the form of monitoring and evaluation activities on the implementation of policies, programs and activities for women's empowerment and child protection.

Article 35

(1) The business sector has responsibilities in the implementation of women's empowerment and child protection.

(2) The form of business sector responsibility as referred to in paragraph (4) is carried out in the form of:

a. financial assistance to service institutions;
b. empowerment;
c. capacity building;
d. publication and information media;
e. provision of facilities and infrastructure;
f. establishment of special institutions for prevention and violence complaint services; and
g. non-binding social assistance.

Article 34

(1) The Regional Government has an obligation to strengthen community participation in in the development of women's empowerment and Child Protection.

(2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out in the form of:

a. socialization;
b. advocacy;
c. counseling;
d. guidance;
e. supervision;
f. facilitation;
g. services;
h. studies;
i. training;
j. capital provision; and
k. provision of facilities and infrastructure.
(3) Strengthening community participation as referred to in paragraph (1)may be carried out by:
a. strengthening cooperation;
b. strengthening institutional capacity for prevention and services from the community, Islamic boarding schools and the business sector; and
c. financing support.

Article 5

(1) Every woman has the right to:

a. live and maintain life and improve her standard of living;
b. fulfill her basic needs to grow and develop properly, form a family and continue descendants;
c. develop her personality, to obtain education, and improve her quality of life to become a faithful, pious, responsible, noble, happy, and prosperous human being in accordance with human rights;
d. obtain justice, security, and freedom to express opinions without discrimination;
e. be involved in every stage of the development process;
f. be free from slavery or servitude and threats;
g. obtain protection of her person, family, honor, dignity and property rights;
h. obtain welfare and a decent life;
i. participate in politics and obtain equal opportunities in government;
j. perform legal acts; and
k. freely choose a partner in her marriage.
(2) In addition to the rights as referred to in paragraph (1), specifically for women with disabilities, they have the right to obtain facilities and special treatment in public services.

Article 27

(1) The Regional Government is responsible for realizing Child Protection in the Region in the form of coordinating the implementation of the administration of Child Protection in the Region.

(2) The coordination of the implementation of the administration of Child Protection in the Region as referred to in paragraph (1) is in order to fulfill the Rights of Children who require Special Protection.

(3) Children who require Special Protection as referred to in paragraph (1) include:

a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are exploited economically and/or sexually; e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children who are perpetrators of pornography;
h. children with human immunodeficiency virus/acquired immuno deficiency syndrome;
i. children victims of abduction, sale, and/or trafficking; j. children victims of physical and/or psychological violence;
k. children victims of sexual crimes;
l. children victims of terrorism networks;
m. children perpetrators of terrorism networks:
n. children with disabilities;
o. children victims of mistreatment and neglect;
p. children with deviant social behavior; and
q. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.

