Article 26

The Regional Government provides facilities to fulfill the Right to Education, Use of Leisure Time and Cultural Activities, by:

a. providing the widest possible opportunity for children to obtain education by ensuring the implementation of a 12 (twelve) year compulsory education program for all children free of charge;
b. providing opportunities and accessibility for children with disabilities to obtain education;
c. organizing inclusive education for children with disabilities; d. optimizing skills education;
e. providing city parks, playgrounds, art buildings and sports arenas as a forum to hone and develop children's talents, interests and creativity in the fields of arts, culture and sports;
f. realizing child-friendly schools at every level of education; g. providing scholarships for students from underprivileged families;
h. facilitating school dropouts at Open Schools or Community Learning Activity Centers;
i. providing awards to students who excel in academics, arts, culture and sports;
j. issuing policies for education providers not to expel students from educational institutions, by conducting guidance, except involved in criminal acts;
k. organizing an educational and non-violent student orientation period; and
l. establishing a children's creativity center.

Article 23

The Regional Government provides facilities to fulfill basic health and welfare rights, by:

a. provision of child-friendly health centers;
b. provision of breastfeeding rooms in Government and private Offices; c. organizing and facilitating Posyandu facilities and infrastructure in every official banjar/neighborhood;
d. provision of clean water;
e. arrangement of green open spaces and a clean, healthy and comfortable environment;
f. provision of smoke-free public spaces; and
g. facilities aimed at reducing maternal mortality rates and child mortality rates as well as increasing life expectancy, nutritional standards and health standards.

Article 20

The Regional Government provides facilities for the fulfillment of children's rights in the family environment and alternative environment care, by: a. providing or facilitating alternative care for children whose parents have died or are sick so that it is not possible to care for the child;

b. providing welfare support to all children; c. fulfilling the rights of child growth and development and protecting children with social welfare problems and children in conflict with the law; d. protecting children from cruel, inhuman treatment and degrading human dignity;

e. conducting training for parents on good parenting or establishing consultation institutions for families;

f. playing an active role in helping families carry out their obligations;

g. preventing marriage at child age; and h. providing child-friendly infrastructure, child-friendly play areas and safe routes to school.

